Persistent Postural-Perceptual Dizziness (PPPD)
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Chronic non-spinning dizziness >3 months, Exacerbation with upright posture, Worsening with complex visual stimuli, Normal vestibular function testing

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-038
Reason for Visit: Severe, acute exacerbation of profound unsteadiness and acute panic leading to functional collapse and inability to stand.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with acute, severe dizziness, profound unsteadiness causing a complete inability to walk, and extreme distress. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively perform a neurological and vestibular examination, exclude an acute posterior circulation stroke or acute vestibular neuritis, and safely manage the acute functional collapse prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head is swimming, I feel like I'm rocking on a boat, and if I stand up I know I'm going to fall over."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute exacerbation of a chronic, fluctuating sensation of non-spinning dizziness and profound unsteadiness present for 6 months. Tonight, the symptoms became severely debilitating following a trip to a highly visually stimulating, busy supermarket. The patient reports severe exacerbation of the dizziness specifically upon assuming an upright posture and when turning the head. There is a complete absence of true severe rotational vertigo (the room is not spinning), profound nystagmus, or acute hearing loss. The patient exhibits extreme anxiety and a significant fear of falling, causing them to crawl to the bedroom.
Functional Status: Currently bedbound, refusing to stand or walk due to intense perceived unsteadiness.
Social/Safety Concerns: Profound fear of falling leading to acute agoraphobia and isolation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 70 Kg
General: Anxious, lying completely flat in a dark room, eyes closed to avoid visual input.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination is globally intact; cranial nerves II-XII normal, power 5/5, normal reflexes. Cerebellar testing (finger-to-nose, heel-to-shin) is completely normal, ruling out acute ataxia. Romberg test is negative when eyes are open and focused, though the patient reports subjective intense swaying. Head Impulse Test (HINTS exam) is completely normal with absolutely no spontaneous or gaze-evoked nystagmus, ruling out acute peripheral or central vestibular lesions. Documented history of entirely normal comprehensive vestibular function testing and a completely normal brain MRI.
Environmental Observations: Curtains tightly drawn, all screens turned off to minimize visual stimulation.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications; avoids meclizine as it 'makes the brain fog worse'.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Persistent Postural-Perceptual Dizziness (PPPD) - Status: Severe acute perceptual flare
Differentials:

Posterior Circulation Stroke/TIA - Ruled out explicitly by the normal HINTS exam (no nystagmus, normal head impulse), normal cranial nerves, and normal cerebellar coordination.

Acute Vestibular Neuritis / Labyrinthitis - Ruled out by the complete lack of severe rotational vertigo, absence of spontaneous nystagmus, and normal hearing.

Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo (BPPV) - Ruled out as the dizziness is continuous/rocking rather than brief (<1 min) intense rotational spinning triggered by specific head positions.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (safely in bed)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to a severe, acute exacerbation of unsteadiness resulting in functional immobility and mimicking an acute neurological emergency (e.g., cerebellar stroke). The inability to walk safely mandated an immediate, hands-on clinical neurological and vestibular examination. By executing a targeted HINTS exam and confirming completely normal cerebellar and structural neurology, the physician safely excluded a life-threatening acute central lesion. This definitive clinical diagnosis of a benign perceptual software error safely aborted a highly distressing psychological crisis and prevented an unnecessary emergency department admission, satisfying the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided immediate, firm clinical reassurance of intact balance hardware (brain and inner ear). Assisted the patient to safely stand and walk down the hallway using graded exposure techniques and physical support.
Positive Behavioral Change: When standing up, intentionally fix your gaze on a single, stationary, solid object across the room (like a picture frame) rather than looking down at your feet or around the room. Providing the brain with a single, highly reliable visual anchor immediately overrides the faulty, hypersensitive motion signals, rewarding the patient with immediate physical stability and reducing the fear of falling.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral to a specialized vestibular physiotherapist for habituation exercises.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to discuss an SSRI/SNRI trial for central neuromodulation of the perceptual pathways.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation or call 000 if developing sudden severe spinning vertigo with vomiting, sudden double vision, slurred speech, or genuine inability to move an arm or leg.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on previously normal structural imaging; if new, true focal neurological signs develop, immediate stroke assessment is required.
Clinical Course: PPPD is a software glitch in how your brain processes balance data, usually triggered by a past inner ear issue or stress. The hardware (ears and brain) is perfectly healthy, but the brain is overreacting to normal movement.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the room suddenly starts violently spinning, if you see double, if your speech becomes slurred, or if your face droops.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your brain and inner ears are completely structurally sound. However, your brain has become hyper-reliant on visual information for balance. The busy supermarket overloaded your visual system, causing a 'system crash' of dizziness. Staying in the dark actually makes this worse by starving the brain of data.
Actionable Steps: Tomorrow, do not stay in bed. You must engage in 10 minutes of gentle walking in a quiet, simple environment. Gradually exposing your brain to normal movement without falling physically forces the 'software' to recalibrate and realize that movement is safe, breaking the cycle of chronic dizziness.

SMS INFORMATION

Diagnosis: PPPD (Perceptual Dizziness) flare. Neuro/ear exam is perfectly normal, NO stroke. Practice looking at a fixed target when walking. See GP in 1 week.

SMS EDUCATION

Fix your eyes on a stationary object when walking to improve stability. Do not stay in bed all day. Call 000 if you get double vision or slurred speech.
